      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's part 2 of 2 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03 revision 10, pages 5-7 of 7. It's a water meter certificate (R49).\n\nThe fragment contains:\n1. Certificate header info (pages 5, 6, 7): Number R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03 revision 10, Project number 3948757, Page X of 7, NMi (Netherlands), OIML Member State: The Netherlands.\n2. Table 4: General characteristics of the indicating device — meter size vs indicating range [m³] vs verification scale interval [m³].\n3. Footnote * about DN600 display indication range.\n4. Table 5: Approved software versions with CRC checksums (continues to page 6).\n5. Certificate history table: revisions with dates and descriptions.\n\nSchema fields to fill:\n- certificate: number, scheme (A), project_number, page_total (7), member_state (The Netherlands), date_issued... The date of issue isn't directly present, but revision 10 date is 5 March 2026. Revision history shows revision 10 dated 5 March 2026. The date_issued isn't explicitly given in this fragment — but the current revision date could be inferred. Rule 8 says don't fabricate. The revision 10 date is \"5 March 2026\" which corresponds to the current revision. Hmm, I could include revision_history with that. For date_issued — not explicitly stated as \"date issued\". I'll omit it or... Actually many extractors would take the revision date as issue date. But to be safe, since this is a fragment, I'll omit date_issued since not explicitly labeled. Actually, the header doesn't show a date. I'll omit.\n\n- oiml_issuer_id: NMi — the \"NL1\" in the number? The issuer ID might be \"NL1\". Not explicit. Omit or include? The certificate number contains \"NL1\". Not explicit elsewhere. I'll omit oiml_issuer_id.\n\n- issuing_authority: name \"NMi\", member state The Netherlands. Address not present. person_responsible not present. So issuing_authority: {name: \"NMi\", oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"?}. Hmm. The scheme is A (from \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\"). Issuer ID... I could put \"NL1\" — it's plausibly the issuer identifier. But rule 8: don't fabricate. \"NL1\" appears in the number; it's reasonable to say oiml_issuer_id is NL1? I think it's safer to omit since it's not labeled. Actually, oiml_issuer_id in the schema — the certificate number format is R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03 where NL1 is the issuer code. I'll include oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" — hmm. Let me be conservative and omit it. Actually, in the OIML CS scheme, the certificate number format is like \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\" where NL1 is the issuing authority identifier. I think including \"NL1\" is a reasonable extraction, not fabrication. But since it's not explicitly labeled as such in the fragment... I'll omit to be safe. Hmm, but the task says extract what's present. The number itself is present, so parsing NL1 out of it is inference. I'll omit.\n\nWait — but recommendation: id R49, edition 2013, scheme A. That's clear from the number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\". R49 is the water meter recommendation. Edition 2013. Accuracy classes: not mentioned in this fragment. Omit accuracy_classes.\n\n- certificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\" — should I include \"revision 10\"? There's revision_history for that. The number as stated: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03 revision 10\". I'll put number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\" and note revision 10 in... the schema doesn't have a revision field at certificate level. Hmm. revision_history covers revisions 0-10. The current revision is 10. Maybe include in number? I'll keep number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\" — the revision is captured in revision_history. Or I could set number to \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03 revision 10\"? Better keep clean number.\n\n- page_total: 7.\n\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\".\n\n- model_family: The Table 4 has meter sizes DN50 through DN600. This is a config/model matrix. The cert covers multiple meter sizes. Models: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250 (till DN350 implies DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350), DN400...DN500, DN600. Actually \"DN200 till DN350\" — sizes in between at DN increments: DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350. And \"DN400 till DN500\": DN400, DN450, DN500. But we don't know exact increments (could be DN50 increments). Safer: model entries as they appear: \"DN50\", \"DN65\", \"DN80\", \"DN100\", \"DN125\", \"DN150\", \"DN200–DN350\", \"DN400–DN500\", \"DN600\"? Hmm.\n\nActually, from part 1 (not given), there's likely a flow characteristics table by meter size (like Table 1/2/3). This fragment only has Table 4 (indicating device) and Table 5 (software versions). Since we only extract for THIS fragment, we populate model_level for indicating range and verification scale interval by meter size.\n\nTable 4 rows:\n- DN50: indicating range 99.999, VSI 0.0001\n- DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125: indicating range 999.999, VSI 0.001\n- DN150: 999.999, VSI 0.01\n- DN200 till DN350: 9.999.999, VSI 0.01\n- DN400 till DN500: 9.999.999, VSI 0.1\n- DN600: 99.999.999* , VSI 0.1\n\nNote: the numbers use dots as thousands separators (European style): \"99.999\" = 99,999? Wait — \"99.999\" with indicating range in m³. For a DN50 water meter, indicating range 99,999 m³ (5 digits before decimal) makes sense. And \"9.999.999\" = 9,999,999 m³. DN600: \"99.999.999\" = 99,999,999 m³ with footnote *.\n\nHmm, but rule says decimal separator is comma in European format. Here dots are thousand separators and commas are decimals: \"0,0001\" = 0.0001, \"0,001\" = 0.001, \"0,01\" = 0.01, \"0,1\" = 0.1. Yes — verification scale intervals are decimals with commas. And indicating ranges use dots as thousands separators: 99.999 → 99999, 999.999 → 999999, 9.999.999 → 9999999, 99.999.999 → 99999999.\n\nSo:\n- DN50: indicating range 99999 m³, VSI 0.0001 m³\n- DN65/DN80/DN100/DN125: 999999 m³, VSI 0.001 m³\n- DN150: 999999 m³, VSI 0.01 m³\n- DN200–DN350: 9999999 m³, VSI 0.01 m³\n- DN400–DN500: 9999999 m³, VSI 0.1 m³\n- DN600: 99999999 m³ (footnote *), VSI 0.1 m³\n\nModels: Should I enumerate each DN size as a model? The model family spans DN50..DN600. For model_level, I'll use conditions like \"DN50\", \"DN65–DN125\", etc. matching the table rows. But better to enumerate the models as given: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200 till DN350 (range), DN400 till DN500, DN600.\n\nI think for model_family.models I should list individual sizes: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600? But \"till DN350\" doesn't guarantee DN50 steps... Actually DN sizes are standard: DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350. It's a strong convention. However, to avoid fabricating, I could use the grouped conditions directly in model_level values:\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- attribute \"indicating_range\", unit \"m³\", values:\n  - {model: \"DN50\", value: 99999}\n  - {model: \"DN65\", value: 999999}\n  - {model: \"DN80\", value: 999999}\n  - {model: \"DN100\", value: 999999}\n  - {model: \"DN125\", value: 999999}\n  - {model: \"DN150\", value: 999999}\n  - {model: \"DN200–DN350\", value: 9999999}\n  - {model: \"DN400–DN500\", value: 9999999}\n  - {model: \"DN600\", value: 99999999, footnote_markers: [\"*\"]}\n- attribute \"verification_scale_interval\", unit \"m³\", values:\n  - {model: \"DN50\", value: 0.0001}\n  - {model: \"DN65\", value: 0.001}\n  - {model: \"DN80\", value: 0.001}\n  - {model: \"DN100\", value: 0.001}\n  - {model: \"DN125\", value: 0.001}\n  - {model: \"DN150\", value: 0.01}\n  - {model: \"DN200–DN350\", value: 0.01}\n  - {model: \"DN400–DN500\", value: 0.1}\n  - {model: \"DN600\", value: 0.1}\n\nHmm, should the model groups be per-row or expanded? Expanding ranges into individual DNs assumes steps. I'll keep groups as conditions (\"DN200–DN350\") to be faithful. Alternatively, keep the raw groups: \"DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125\" as one condition. Hmm. The schema example used individual model_ids. Let me use model entries per row-group and expand the explicit list \"DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125\" into four entries